<chapter>

<title><classname>HttpdDataSource</classname> Reference</title>
 <section id="HttpdDataSource" xreflabel="HttpdDataSource">
  <title>Introduction</title>
  <para>
  The <classname>HttpdDataSource</classname> is a base class that represents
  a source of data. Examples are things like flash chips, files, and memory 
  buffers.
  </para>

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Methods</title>

  <section><title><function>ReadAt</function> (Generic)</title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       int
       <function><classname>HttpdDataSource</classname>::ReadAt</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>void *<parameter>p_data</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>size_t <parameter>sz</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>unsigned long <parameter>offset</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This pure virtual function is the interface for reading data from
    the source. On success, <parameter>sz</parameter> bytes are written
    to the buffer pointed to by <parameter>p_data</parameter> starting at
    <parameter>offset</parameter> bytes from the start of the data from the
    source.
   </para>

   <para>
    Upon success, <literal>0</literal> is returned; otherwise a system
    dependent error value is returned (see <xref
    linkend="opsErrorCodes">).
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>ReadAt</function> (unsigned long)</title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       int
       <function><classname>HttpdDataSource</classname>::ReadAt</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>unsigned long &amp;<parameter>val</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>unsigned long <parameter>offset</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This method reads an unsigned long value (at least 32-bits in length)
    from the source starting at offset <parameter>offset</parameter> from
    the start of the data in the source.
   </para>
   
   <para>
    Upon success, <literal>0</literal> is returned; otherwise a system
    dependent error value is returned (see <xref
    linkend="opsErrorCodes">).
   </para>

  </section>


  <section><title><function>ReadAt</function> (unsigned short)</title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       int
       <function><classname>HttpdDataSource</classname>::ReadAt</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>unsigned short &amp;<parameter>val</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>unsigned long <parameter>offset</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This method reads an unsigned short value (at least 16-bits in length)
    from the source starting at offset <parameter>offset</parameter> from
    the start of the data in the source.
   </para>
   
   <para>
    Upon success, <literal>0</literal> is returned; otherwise a system
    dependent error value is returned (see <xref
    linkend="opsErrorCodes">).
   </para>

  </section>


  <section><title><function>AddressOf</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       const void *
       <function><classname>HttpdDataSource</classname>::AddressOf</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>unsigned long <parameter>offset</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>size_t <parameter>sz</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Some sources of data are accessible directly via a memory address.
    For those kinds of sources this method allows access to the memory
    space. If a <classname>HttpdDataSource</classname> does not support
    access via a pointer this function can safely return 
    <symbol>NULL</symbol> and the data will be accessed using the 
    <function>ReadAt</function> methods.
   </para>

   <para>
    If the data can be mapped for <parameter>sz</parameter> bytes
    starting at <parameter>offset</parameter> from the start of the data in
    the source, the mapped address should be returned. The returned address
    should be valid until <function>ReleaseAddress</function> is called on
    the returned pointer.
   </para>

   <para>
    This function should only be implemented for cases where access through
    a pointer would be faster than calls to <function>ReadAt</function>. 
    For example, allocating a buffer and reading the contents of a file into
    it is not really any more efficient than having the data read into a 
    buffer provided to <function>ReadAt</function>.
    </para>
 
    <para>
      However, something like a Disk-On-Chip with a memory-mapping window 
      would be appropriate.
    </para>
  </section>

  <section><title><function>ReleaseAddress</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       <function><classname>HttpdDataSource</classname>::ReleaseAddress</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const void *<parameter>p_addr</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
   This method releases any resources associated with a mapped address
   obtained from <function>AddressOf</function>. 
   </para>

  </section>

 </section>

</chapter>

